# __PROJECT_NAME__

Generated from [goscaffoldforlevi](https://github.com/namezzy/goscaffoldforlevi).

## Quick start

Requires Go 1.27+ and Docker.

```sh
cp .env.example .env      # every value is a working default
make tools                # pinned golangci-lint, sqlc, migrate, govulncheck
docker compose up -d      # postgres + redis
make migrate-up
make run
```

Then `curl localhost:8080/ready` reports each dependency by name.

Create the first administrator — the API cannot bootstrap itself, because
`POST /users` requires the `user:create` permission:

```sh
make bootstrap-admin email=you@example.com
```

## What you inherited

| Area | What is already here |
|---|---|
| HTTP | chi, strict JSON decoding, PATCH-only updates, one error shape, `X-Request-ID` on every response |
| Persistence | pgx pool, sqlc queries, golang-migrate, transactions hidden behind a context key |
| Auth | Argon2id, JWT access tokens, opaque rotating refresh tokens with theft detection, RBAC |
| Rate limiting | Redis token bucket; general scopes fail open, login fails closed |
| Messaging | Transactional outbox and a NATS consumer, off until `MESSAGING_ENABLED=true` |
| Observability | OpenTelemetry traces, Prometheus metrics on a separate port, `trace_id` in every log line |
| Delivery | Dockerfile (distroless), GitHub Actions, Kustomize manifests for four environments |

The reasoning behind each of these is in `docs/adr/` — ten decisions, with the
alternatives that were rejected and the conditions that should make you revisit
them. `docs/architecture.md` covers what did not need an ADR.

## Adding a feature

```sh
goscaffold module order          # domain, service, ports, HTTP adapter, tests
goscaffold migration create_orders
```

The generator writes new files and never touches existing ones, so wiring the
module into `cmd/api/features.go`, registering its permissions in
`internal/auth/policy.go` and documenting it in `api/openapi.yaml` are yours to
do. It prints the list when it finishes.

## Gates

```sh
make check              # fmt, vet, lint, test -race, govulncheck, sqlc-check
make test-integration   # testcontainers; needs Docker
```

Several conventions in this project are enforced by tests rather than by
documentation: the dependency direction, the error-kind to HTTP-status table,
`.env.example` staying in step with the config loader, the router and
`api/openapi.yaml` agreeing in both directions, and the Kubernetes manifests
keeping their shutdown budget and probe paths. When one of them fails, the
message says which rule and why it exists.
